Windmc reads its input as raw bytes and picks the encoding solely from the
command line: -a/-C for a code page, -u for UTF-16, defaulting to code page
1252. It never examines a byte order mark. Windows text editors and the
Microsoft mc.exe toolchain routinely emit .mc files with one, and every
such file is rejected:

* A UTF-8 BOM, EF BB BF, decodes under code page 1252 as U+00EF U+00BB
  U+00BF. All three are >= 0x40, so the lexer glues them into an
  identifier token and the parse dies with "syntax error" on line 1, with
  no hint that the encoding is at fault.

* A UTF-16LE BOM, FF FE, is U+FEFF, likewise >= 0x40 and likewise a stray
  identifier, so even the correct "windmc -u" fails the same way. Without
  -u the file is decoded as code page 1252 mojibake instead.

Detect and consume both marks. A byte order mark identifies the encoding
authoritatively, so let it override the switches rather than the other way
around, which is also how mc.exe behaves. UTF-16BE is not handled: it
would require byte swapping the whole input, and it does not occur in
practice.

--- a/binutils/windmc.c
+++ b/binutils/windmc.c
@@ -1109,3 +1109,3 @@
     rc_uint_type ul;
-    char *buff;
+    char *buff, *cont;
     bfd_size_type flen;
@@ -1124,5 +1124,21 @@
     fclose (fp);
+    /* A byte order mark identifies the encoding authoritatively, so
+       consume it and let it override the command line switches.  */
+    cont = buff;
+    if (flen >= 3 && ! memcmp (cont, "\357\273\277", 3))
+      {
+	cont += 3;
+	flen -= 3;
+	mcset_text_in_is_unicode = 0;
+	mcset_codepage_in = CP_UTF8;
+      }
+    else if (flen >= 2 && ! memcmp (cont, "\377\376", 2))
+      {
+	cont += 2;
+	flen -= 2;
+	mcset_text_in_is_unicode = 1;
+      }
     if (mcset_text_in_is_unicode != 1)
       {
-	unicode_from_codepage (&ul, &u, buff, mcset_codepage_in);
+	unicode_from_codepage (&ul, &u, cont, mcset_codepage_in);
 	if (! u)
@@ -1135,3 +1151,3 @@
 	  fatal (_("input file does not seems to be UFT16.\n"));
-	mc_set_content ((unichar *) buff);
+	mc_set_content ((unichar *) cont);
       }
